1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,650
Point a camera at its own screen, and the
machine starts looking at itself.

2
00:00:10,950 --> 00:00:15,175
What comes back is the closest thing a machine
has to a dream.

3
00:00:59,125 --> 00:01:02,550
Before cinema, before art, there was the eye.

4
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:08,181
It began as almost nothing: a patch of
light-sensitive cells that could tell day from

5
00:01:08,181 --> 00:01:08,575
night.

6
00:01:08,875 --> 00:01:13,500
Fold that patch into a cup, and it can tell
where the light is coming from.

7
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:17,375
Narrow the cup into a pinhole, and an image
appears.

8
00:01:17,675 --> 00:01:20,675
Fill it with a lens, and the image sharpens.

9
00:01:20,975 --> 00:01:27,107
In 1994, the biologists Dan-Eric Nilsson and
Susanne Pelger estimated that this could

10
00:01:27,107 --> 00:01:31,075
happen in fewer than four hundred thousand
generations.

11
00:01:31,375 --> 00:01:34,225
In the history of life, that's a blink.

12
00:01:40,958 --> 00:01:47,133
Before anyone said psychedelic, cinema's first
magicians were already bending reality.

13
00:03:36,958 --> 00:03:39,083
Your eyes are fish eyes.

14
00:03:39,133 --> 00:03:45,205
Around five hundred million years ago, early
jawless fish already had camera eyes, with a

15
00:03:45,205 --> 00:03:48,183
lens, a retina, and muscles to aim them.

16
00:03:48,233 --> 00:03:51,583
We inherited the design, flaws included.

17
00:03:51,633 --> 00:03:57,011
Our retina is wired backwards: its nerves run
in front of the light-sensitive cells, and

18
00:03:57,011 --> 00:04:00,683
where they leave the eye there's a hole, the
blind spot.

19
00:04:00,733 --> 00:04:04,933
Fish, reptiles and birds have four kinds of
color cells.

20
00:04:04,983 --> 00:04:10,433
Our mammal ancestors, living at night in the
age of the dinosaurs, lost two.

21
00:04:10,483 --> 00:04:13,483
Our primate ancestors won one back.

22
00:04:13,533 --> 00:04:16,283
That's why you see the colors you see.

23
00:04:23,042 --> 00:04:27,917
Then painters picked up the camera, and film
became music for the eyes.

24
00:07:35,042 --> 00:07:38,717
And the picture you see isn't what your eyes
record.

25
00:07:38,767 --> 00:07:43,617
Your eyes jump about three times a second, and
your brain hides the jumps.

26
00:07:43,667 --> 00:07:46,542
It also paints over your blind spot.

27
00:07:46,592 --> 00:07:51,904
Try it: close your left eye, stare at the
cross, and lean toward the screen until the

28
00:07:51,904 --> 00:07:53,092
dot disappears.

29
00:07:59,125 --> 00:08:02,425
And it turns separate pictures into motion.

30
00:08:02,475 --> 00:08:08,425
Show it a series of still photographs, like
Eadweard Muybridge's galloping horse from

31
00:08:08,425 --> 00:08:10,775
1878, and it invents movement.

32
00:08:10,825 --> 00:08:14,225
Every film in this story depends on that
trick.

33
00:08:21,792 --> 00:08:24,642
And the surrealists filmed their dreams.

34
00:11:50,083 --> 00:11:54,233
Philosophers were worried about seeing long
before cinema.

35
00:11:54,283 --> 00:12:01,383
Around 375 BC, Plato imagined prisoners
chained in a cave, facing a wall.

36
00:12:01,433 --> 00:12:07,283
Behind them burns a fire, and people carry
objects past it, so the prisoners see only

37
00:12:07,283 --> 00:12:07,833
shadows.

38
00:12:08,133 --> 00:12:10,458
They take the shadows for the world.

39
00:12:10,758 --> 00:12:15,798
It's the oldest description of a movie
theater, and a warning: what you see may be a

40
00:12:15,798 --> 00:12:16,708
projection.

41
00:12:47,667 --> 00:12:49,842
Then came the light show.

42
00:12:49,892 --> 00:12:52,367
Images, performed live.

43
00:14:17,333 --> 00:14:23,127
Around 1790, the poet William Blake wrote that
if the doors of perception were cleansed,

44
00:14:23,127 --> 00:14:25,958
everything would appear as it is: infinite.

45
00:14:26,258 --> 00:14:31,653
In 1953, the writer Aldous Huxley took
mescaline with the psychiatrist Humphry

46
00:14:31,653 --> 00:14:36,883
Osmond, and borrowed Blake's phrase for a
book: The Doors of Perception.

47
00:14:36,933 --> 00:14:43,133
A few years later, Osmond proposed a new word
for drugs that seemed to reveal the mind.

48
00:14:43,183 --> 00:14:44,983
Psychedelic.

49
00:14:45,033 --> 00:14:47,933
From the Greek: mind-manifesting.

50
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:59,050
Then the television became an instrument.

51
00:16:52,667 --> 00:16:55,367
Then the edit became a magic trick.

52
00:18:52,417 --> 00:18:56,242
Then the dream machine moved into the home
computer.

53
00:20:55,958 --> 00:21:02,788
In 1637, René Descartes described cutting away
the back of an ox's eye, and seeing the world

54
00:21:02,788 --> 00:21:05,933
projected on it, small and upside down.

55
00:21:05,983 --> 00:21:07,958
But that raised a puzzle.

56
00:21:08,258 --> 00:21:11,583
If the eye makes a picture, who looks at the
picture?

57
00:21:11,883 --> 00:21:14,283
A little person inside the head?

58
00:21:14,583 --> 00:21:18,058
Then who looks inside that little person's
head?

59
00:21:18,108 --> 00:21:23,383
Philosophers call it an infinite regress, and
it looks a lot like video feedback.

60
00:21:23,683 --> 00:21:28,889
Today, many neuroscientists say the brain
doesn't watch a picture at all: it predicts

61
00:21:28,889 --> 00:21:31,583
one, and checks its guess against the light.

62
00:21:31,883 --> 00:21:35,958
Perception, in their phrase, is a controlled
hallucination.

63
00:21:36,258 --> 00:21:38,683
Which brings us to the machines.

64
00:22:12,250 --> 00:22:14,975
Now the machines dream on their own.

65
00:22:59,750 --> 00:23:02,750
The artist's job is to stay in the loop.

66
00:23:23,250 --> 00:23:27,350
Point your phone's camera at a screen showing
its own picture.

67
00:23:27,400 --> 00:23:29,400
You're part of the loop.
